**All exportable annotation queues are empty:** 0 dictionary tasks and 0 contextual homonym tasks.

However, the broader check found unresolved cases outside those queues:

- **Partially resolved 2026-10-07:** of the 15,996 unreviewed native-labelled mixed-harmony forms,
  7,173 whose native and loanword conversion branches agree now convert automatically during training export.
  Of the 8,823 origin-dependent forms, 111 across 36 families were recovered from existing direct human reviews,
  preserving human spellings, variants, and source/analyzer provenance. The remaining 8,712 forms stay excluded
  from annotation queues and training export. All pre-existing review rows were retained unchanged.
  After recovery, all 47,259 reviewed words and 479 variants pass dictionary validation.
  Entries, validation, manifest, and full database backup: [docs/mixed_harmony_family_recovery.md](docs/mixed_harmony_family_recovery.md).
- **Resolved 2026-10-07:** all 114 stored `HAMZA` reviews were migrated to preserved `ʼ` (U+02BC) spellings.
  Preferred policy: preserve hamza wherever possible. All other reviewed text and origins were retained.
  At that migration, all 47,148 reviewed words and 478 reviewed variants passed dictionary validation.
  Original entries, migrated spellings, and backup location: [docs/hamza_stored_reviews.md](docs/hamza_stored_reviews.md).
  Converter updated 2026-10-07: verified hamza families preserve `ʼ` deterministically in both origin branches, suffix forms, and hyphenated components; the retired DSL/project stays removed.

Earlier completion counts covered the exported queues only. So the current projects are complete, but I can’t yet confirm that the entire dataset needs no further review or cleanup.

